body:
External template library by path: a deck's template library need not live in a local `templates/` directory. `kalide.yaml` may set the optional top-level key `templates:` to a path naming the library directory to use; that directory has exactly the same fixed layout as a local `templates/` (templates-dir-layout) and the layout itself is unchanged — only its root is resolved. A library is load-valid with no templates and no themes: nothing beyond `library.yaml` plus that layout structure is required. A deck, however, always resolves a theme — `theme` is optional and defaults to `default` (deck-config, theme-selection) — against the resolved library, and an unresolvable theme is an error; so a deck pointed at a library that defines no matching theme fails validation, and a library that is to host a deck must provide the deck's theme (the `default` theme is sufficient). Path semantics: a relative path is resolved against the deck root (the directory holding `kalide.yaml`), for portability; an absolute path is allowed. Exactly one library per deck: when the key is present it is authoritative and wins; a local `templates/` is then ignored. When the key is absent, kalide falls back to a local `templates/`. kalide never silently falls back from a configured path to a local `templates/`, and a configured path that is missing or not a valid library is a templates error naming the path (templates-dir-required). When neither resolves, the error names the expected local `templates/`. The resolved root is used everywhere a local `templates/` is used today (load, validate, render, serve, the `kalide templates` gallery and live reload), always through the template-library-loader. Git and network are out of scope: kalide never clones, fetches or otherwise obtains a library — the user supplies the directory and kalide only references it by path (this realizes global.constraint.template-library-shareable). `kalide init <path>` can point a new deck at an external library, validating the library AND the deck's theme against it before writing; `kalide init-library <path>` creates a new library that seeds a minimal `default` theme so a defaulted deck resolves (see cli-init, init-library).

AC:
- a deck with `templates: ../shared-lib` and no local `templates/` loads, validates, serves, live-reloads and lists in `kalide templates` from `../shared-lib`;
- a deck with both `templates: ../shared-lib` and a local `templates/` uses `../shared-lib` and ignores the local dir;
- a deck with neither the key nor a local `templates/` fails (non-zero exit) with a message naming the expected local `templates/` and suggesting `kalide init`, and no fallback;
- a deck whose `templates:` path is missing or is not a valid library (bad/missing `library.yaml`, layout violation) fails with a templates error naming the resolved path;
- a deck whose theme (optional, default `default`) does not resolve in the resolved library fails validation (theme-selection);
- `kalide init ../shared-lib` against a library that resolves the deck's theme (default `default`) — which need not define any template — writes an empty `slides/`, empty `assets/` and `kalide.yaml` (with `templates: ../shared-lib`), NO local `templates/` and NO starter slide, and `kalide start` validates and serves from `../shared-lib` (the deck's slide set may be empty); `kalide init ../bad-lib` (invalid library, or a library that does not resolve the deck's theme) fails before writing anything.
